namespace middleware.ratelimit;

import redis;

/// 60 requests per rolling 60s window, keyed on the client address.
///
/// `redis.rate_limit` is INCR + EXPIRE in one Lua script. The hand-rolled
/// read-modify-write this replaced got two things wrong: concurrent
/// requests could both read the same count and both write count+1, and the
/// window was per-process, so N replicas meant an effective limit of 60xN.
///
/// `request.client_ip()` reads `X-Forwarded-For` only through the
/// `trusted_proxies` list in `app.jwc`; with none configured it is the
/// socket peer. That is the whole rule (config.md §3.3) and it is why this
/// no longer hand-reads `cf-connecting-ip`.
/// **1.0 has no in-process fallback**, unlike the 0.9 package: without a
/// server every `redis.*` name but `enabled()` raises. Unguarded, this
/// middleware answers 500 to every request of a deployment that has no
/// Redis — including the local `docker compose` loop with the container
/// stopped. A shortener over its rate limit is a nuisance, not a
/// correctness problem, so the call is guarded and a Redis-less
/// deployment serves.
///
/// It does not pretend to be rate limited either. `jwc_redis_pool_*` in
/// `/metrics` appears only when Redis is configured, which is how an
/// operator tells the two apart.
///
/// Written as an `if` around the work rather than an early exit: falling
/// off the end is how a middleware lets the chain continue
/// (middleware.md §4), and a bare `return;` in the request phase is
/// **not** that — it answers 204 and stops.
middleware RateLimit {
    if (redis.enabled()) {
        let ip = request.client_ip();
        let allowed = redis.rate_limit("rl:" + string.of(@ip), 60, 60);

        if (!@allowed) {
            throw TooManyRequests("juda ko'p so'rov");
        }
    }
}
